00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.110 align:start position:0%
it's rainy season in the amazon normally  a time when logging activities and fires  decrease  yet deforestation has been on the rise  during the first three months of this  year  941 square kilometers of the brazilian  rainforest were destroyed  64 percent more than the same period in  2021  official data just released shows first  quarter deforestation at its most  extensive since 2015.  it's the result of illegal logging and  mining but environmentalists also blame  brazil's president jair bolsonaro  we know that this is a consequence of  several um  of the the environmental policy that has  been implemented by the current  government since it took office in 2019  so the surveillance activities were  drastically reduced there is no action  plan to fight deforestation  uh there are several bills that actually  send the wrong message that the people  who who do environmental crime will not  be punished on the other hand they will  they might be benefited with a change in  the current regulation so all of this  leads to a scenario in which  deforestation rates are out of control  again  bolsonaro is pushing for legislation  that will open indigenous lands to  mining  thousands of representatives of more  than 170  indigenous tribes are now in the capital  brasilia protesting against a set of  bills which will make it easier for  miners loggers and farmers to  commercially exploit the amazon  from january to march deforestation in  indigenous lands increased by 52 percent  compared to the same period last year  environmentalists say this is  particularly concerning because those  areas should be protected whereas today  the opposite is true  monika yanaki of al jazeera rio de  janeiro
 

